  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday Night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1121 AM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nFor now, the heat headlines will continue through tomorrow evening \nand then the question becomes what to do with Monday. Models are \npretty consistent with the upper ridge being squashed to the south \non Monday and a frontal boundary approaching the mid-state by Monday \nafternoon. This may act to keep heat index values more in the \nadvisory levels (105-109 degrees). The other thing that could occur \nwith the boundary approaching is increased chances for storms. Right \nnow, we're only carrying medium chances for storms, however this \nmight change. I'm looking over forecast soundings and the timing of \nthe boundary and I think we could end up seeing more widespread \nstorm chances. Healthy mid-level lapse rates, (obviously) plenty of \ninstability and 4-digit DCAPE values all smell like a wind threat to \nme. The one thing going against this is a lack of vertical wind \nshear. I'm only seeing 10-15 kts of deep layer shear and double \ndigit helicity values. We're going to get into the CAMs tonight and \nI think Monday's picture will become a little clearer tomorrow \nmorning.\n\nA brief respite from the heat should arrive for Tuesday. Something a \nlittle closer to normal values for summer, but as Wednesday rolls \naround and we get back to a southerly boundary layer flow, some of \nthat heat returns. Don't know if we'll see advisory level numbers, \nbut something we'll need to monitor.\n\nBy the end of next week, models are actually in really good \nagreement right now that temperatures will finally return to normal \nfor a while. Here's the catch: it could come with a return in more \nwidespread rain chances. We'll have to see how it pans out, but a \nlarge scale trough could help us get out of this awful heat pattern \nby next weekend.",
